{"seconds": 60.5, "exit": 0, "final": "After Qd5+ White can play Rxd5 or cxd5, losing the queen. So the Rxe7 line is worse for Black. Therefore, **Qd4** is the best move, forcing White into a position where Black wins material.\n\nLet me write the coaching note and verify the main line once more:\n\n**Best move: Qd4**\n\n**Main line:**\n1. ...Qd4 (attacking the rook on d7, the rook on d1, and the bishop)\n2. Rd8+ Rxd8 (Black captures, winning a full rook)\n\nThis is a classic deflection/overloading theme: the Rd7 is overworked defending the back rank and must check, allowing Black to win it by recapture.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Qd4\", \"line\": [\"Qd4\", \"Rd8+\", \"Rxd8\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\", \"deflection\"]}\n```\n\n**Coaching Note (78 words):**\nWhen your opponent's pieces are concentrated in one area, look for moves that attack multiple defenders at once. Here, Qd4 attacks three pieces: the rook on d7 (your target), the rook on d1, and the bishop on c3. White's rook is overloaded\u2014if it stays put, you capture it; if it checks with Rd8+, you recapture and win a full rook. This is called a deflection: forcing a defender away so you can capture the main target. Always ask: \"Is the defender busy with something else?\"", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "1r4k1/1n1Rr2p/4p1p1/2q1PpP1/2P2P1P/p1B5/P3Q1K1/3R4 b - - 1 41"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "1r4k1/1n1Rr2p/4p1p1/2q1PpP1/2P2P1P/p1B5/P3Q1K1/3R4 b - - 1 41"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "1r4k1/1n1Rr2p/4p1p1/2q1PpP1/2P2P1P/p1B5/P3Q1K1/3R4 b - - 1 41", "move": "Qc2"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "1r4k1/1n1Rr2p/4p1p1/2q1PpP1/2P2P1P/p1B5/P3Q1K1/3R4 b - - 1 41", "move": "Qf2"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "1r4k1/1n1Rr2p/4p1p1/2q1PpP1/2P2P1P/p1B5/P3Q1K1/3R4 b - - 1 41", "move": "Qg1"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "1